    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required rejecting a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with HTTP 400.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java:415`): added a guard that returns `400 Bad Request` when a `registrationDate` is supplied and is after `LocalDate.now()`:\n\n```java\nif (owner.getRegistrationDate() != null\n    && owner.getRegistrationDate().isAfter(java.time.LocalDate.now())) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);\n}\n```\n\nI placed this check on the **supplied** value, before the existing null-defaulting / business-day-rolling logic, so:\n- A future date is rejected outright.\n- A `null` date (the optional case) still defaults to the server date as before.\n- The `toBusinessDay` roll-forward still applies only to valid, non-future dates.\n\nThis follows the controller's established idiom of returning `ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)` directly for business-rule failures (matching the postcode/telephone/email/address checks), rather than a Bean Validation annotation.\n\n**Tests:** `./mvnw test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the `errorRejectsFutureDate` acceptance test now passes).",
